# SYNTHESISED to exercise the stall annotation's own truncation and
# ordering: four solo stall windows of DISTINCT duration (5.7s, 5s, 4s,
# 2s), back to back with no gap between them, all comfortably clearing
# stallAnnotation's 1s/5% threshold. The annotation shows only the top
# three by duration, so this fixture pins that the correct three survive
# (5.7s, 5s, 4s) and the shortest (2s) is the one dropped -- a distinction
# testdata/timeline.log cannot exercise, since it clears the threshold
# with only one stall. All boundary times are round multiples of 100ms so
# formatMs's one-decimal rendering has nothing to round.
#
# The one aws span opened before the google span ends up as PackLanes'
# lane 0, and every later aws span reuses that same lane -- PackLanes
# checks lanes in order and lane 0 is always free again by the time the
# next aws span starts -- so lane 0 stays aws-only and every stall here
# names "aws/1", not "mixed/1" (a mixed lane is what an earlier, less
# careful version of this fixture produced when the SHORT google span was
# opened first instead of the long-running aws sequence: PackLanes then
# reused google's lane for the LATER aws spans, since it freed up first,
# which would have made this fixture assert something other than what it
# was built to test). The google span's own lane is never reused, since
# lane 0 is always tried first and always available by the time anything
# else needs a lane.
#
# The line shape is testdata/two-providers.log's "Received downstream
# response" verbatim. The first line is not itself a response: it anchors
# the scanner's base clock a few seconds before the first real span, so
# that span's start is not clamped to zero by ReportedBuilder (see the
# doc comment on span.Span). Values are invented; no line here is from a
# real run.
